#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:4
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &kturtle;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: using-kturtle.docbook:7
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here is a screenshot of &kturtle; in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: using-kturtle.docbook:13
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kturtle; Main Window"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The main window of &kturtle; has three main parts: <link linkend=\"the-code-"
"editor\">the code editor</link> (3) on the left where you type the "
"&turtlelang; commands, the <link linkend=\"the-inspector\">inspector</link> "
"(4) which gives you information about variables when the program runs, and "
"<link linkend=\"the-canvas\">the canvas</link> (5) on the right where the "
"instructions are visualized. <link linkend=\"the-canvas\">The canvas</link> "
"is the turtle's playground: it is on the canvas that the turtle actually "
"moves and draws. The three other places on the main window are: <link "
"linkend=\"the-menubar\">the menu bar</link> (1) from where all the actions "
"can be reached, <link linkend=\"the-toolbar\">the toolbar</link> (2) that "
"allows you to quickly select the most used actions, and <link linkend=\"the-"
"statusbar\">the statusbar</link> (6) where you will find feedback on the "
"state of &kturtle;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:32
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Code Editor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:33
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the code editor you type the &turtlelang; commands. It has all of the "
"features you would expect from a modern editor. Most of its features are "
"found in the <link linkend=\"the-edit-menu\">Edit</link> and the <link "
"linkend=\"the-tools-menu\">Tools</link> menus. The code editor can be docked "
"on each border of the main window or it can be detached and placed anywhere "
"on your desktop."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:34
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You have several ways to get some code in the editor. The easiest way is to "
"use an already-made example: choose <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Examples</guimenuitem> </menuchoice> in the <link "
"linkend=\"the-file-menu\">File menu</link> and select an example. The file "
"example you choose will be opened in the <link linkend=\"the-code-editor"
"\">the code editor</link>, you can then use <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Run</guimenuitem> </menuchoice> to run the code if you "
"like."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:38
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can open &turtlelang; files by choosing <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Open...</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:40
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The third way is to directly type your own code in the editor or to copy/"
"paste some code from this user guide."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The cursor position is indicated in <link linkend=\"the-statusbar\">the "
"statusbar</link>, on the right with the Line number and Column number."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:51
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Inspector"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid "The inspector informs you about the variables while the program is running."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:55
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The inspector can be docked on each border of the main window or it can be "
"detached and placed anywhere on your desktop."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:60
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Canvas"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:61
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The canvas is the area where the commands are visualized, where the commands "
"<quote>draw</quote> a picture. In other words, it is the turtle's "
"playground. After getting some code in the <link linkend=\"the-code-editor"
"\">the code editor</link>, and executing it using <menuchoice><guimenu>File</"
"guimenu><guimenuitem>Run</guimenuitem> </menuchoice>, two things can happen: "
"either the code executes fine, and will you most likely see something change "
"on the canvas; or you have made an error in your code and there will be a "
"message telling you what error you made."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:64
#, no-c-format
msgid "This message should help you to resolve the error."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid "You can zoom in and out the canvas with your mouse wheel."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: using-kturtle.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Menubar"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: using-kturtle.docbook:70
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the menubar you find all the actions of &kturtle;. They are in the "
"following groups: <guimenu>File</guimenu>, <guimenu>Edit</guimenu>, "
"<guimenu>View</guimenu>, <guimenu>Tools</guimenu>, <guimenu>Settings</"
"guimenu>, and <guimenu>Help</guimenu>. This section describes them all."
msgstr ""

